Hello Mohammad

What more can I say? If you need higher authority --
"The combination of some data and an aching desire for an answer does not ensure that a reasonable answer can be extracted from a given body of data" J. Tukey
"...too many ecologists think that complex analytical methods can extract meaningful results from any dataset, irrespective of how those data were collected" J. Millspaugh & R. A. Gitzen

Stating the obvious: If different plausible models give very different answers then you know you have a problem. Just selecting one you like doesn't seem right. If the extrapolation is unpleasing, did you possibly select a hotspot of leopard density for the survey?

Murray

On Monday, October 14, 2019 at 3:14:18 AM UTC+13, Mohammad Farhadinia wrote:
> Hi all
> I used two different methods for analysis:
>
> First, I used the secr.fit with HN function, density and N looked reasonable (around 10 individuals for a D of around 2).
>
> Then, I used both secr.fit and anisotropic.fit (with HNN detection function) and used AIC, the anisotropic ranked highest, but the D increased to around 6 and the N to some where around 40 individuals, which both are unreasonably high for an extremely desert area.
> BTW, the Ф was around 2 in the anisotropic predictions.
>
> I understand why the density has increased in anisotropic, perhaps because it has compressed the long sigmas, causing a smaller ETA, and higher density.
>
> I just wanted to know your thoughts on these for any possible suggestion to improve them. I think I need to exclude the anisotropic and just stick to the original secr.fit results.
>
> Mohammad
>
>
>
>
> On Sunday, October 6, 2019 at 11:53:54 AM UTC+1, Mohammad Farhadinia wrote:
> > Thanks Murray and Eric, very useful comments.
> > Mohammad
> >
> > On Friday, October 4, 2019 at 9:50:23 PM UTC+1, Murray Efford wrote:
> > > For decision makers: "We photographed 8 different leopards in 2012 and 5 in 2016, and know quite a lot about these individuals. However, we are unable to apply the methods of statistical ecology to extrapolate from these known animals to the number and distribution of undetected leopards, and hence to estimate the total population. This is because the numbers are small, the scale of individual movements was large relative to the area we surveyed, the topography constrained sampling and movements preventing us from reliably extrapolating to a 2-dimensional area, etc. Fancy modelling cannot change these fundamentals."
> > >
> > > On Wednesday, October 2, 2019 at 2:01:32 AM UTC+13, Mohammad Farhadinia wrote:
> > > > Hello Murray
> > > > Only males (2 in 2012 and 1 in 2016) had such long excursion, occasionally. But in terms of few numbers detected, I presume open models are not helpful.
> > > >
> > > > My two major concerns are still there:
> > > >
> > > > First, how to report higher density when fewer leopard detected, to decision makers? What should I do to estimate a proper density?
> > > >
> > > > Second, the g0 of 0.005 still looks very low, in similar studies on leopards in Iran we got g0 of around 15-20%.
